														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4468791">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+Opinion</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I'm a bit conflicted on this one. On one hand, this entry satisfies the contest theme nigh-perfectly, giving the reader just enough clues to start formulating their own theories about the anomaly, and the implication of the actual anomaly itself being some sort of infohazardous religion that the Foundation <em>banished to the informational afterlife</em>, combined with some absolutely fantastic lines throughout the article like &quot;Any realised iconography is to be extracted from the cerebellum and incinerated&quot; and &quot;Subtle short-term procedures are also planned, [DATA EXPUNGED] increase biological similarity between cerebrospinal fluid and holy water to lower death toll&quot;, had me finishing the article feeling extremely intrigued and curious. On the other hand&#8230; I feel like this is an instance of leaving <em>too much</em> up to the reader, and having the article suffer for that. You present us with this extremely interesting premise, but the lack of any definitive clues ultimately makes the creation of my own interpretations feel unsatisfying, since I don't really have much to base my interpretations off of. Even just one hint towards the actual nature of the anomaly would've been enough to push me towards an upvote, but given the nature of the anomaly itself, I understand if that's not really possible. In the end, I guess it just comes down to personal preference, and what each individual reader necessarily wants out of the idea of a &quot;mystery&quot;. No-vote, but I may change my mind after further consideration or after the contest is over.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

